PLACES=[
# ---- Concord: North Bridge unit ----
N('old-north-bridge-concord','Old North Bridge and Battle Monument','Concord','bos','landmark','standing',42.46904,-71.35062,'exact','public','walk','','1956 bridge; monument 1836; statue 1875',
  'The wooden bridge over the Concord River where about 400 provincial minutemen and militia advanced on roughly 90 British soldiers on April 19, 1775, the first time American forces advanced in formation on the regulars and routed them. Emerson called it "the shot heard round the world". At least eight bridges have stood here; the present footbridge is an approximate replica built in 1956 and much restored in 2005.',
  'The bridge itself, the 1836 Battle Monument obelisk (designed by Solomon Willard) at the old east abutment, the graves of two British soldiers killed in the fight, and Daniel Chester French\'s 1875 Minute Man statue on the far bank, with Emerson\'s stanza on its pedestal. All of it is in the North Bridge unit of Minute Man National Historical Park.',
  'Where the war began: the bridge, the obelisk and French\'s Minute Man.',
  accn='Minute Man National Historical Park (National Park Service). The park\'s grounds are open daily, year round, sunrise to sunset (NPS hours page). Parking at 280 Monument Street (NPS gives 42.469196, -71.348334) or at the North Bridge Visitor Center lot, 174 Liberty Street, open daily 10 a.m. to 5 p.m. through October 31, 2026. The Trustees\' Old Manse page (read November 2026) mentions National Park Service construction at the North Bridge: check the park\'s alerts before going.',
  links=[W('Old North Bridge'),MIMA,['NPS: operating hours','https://www.nps.gov/mima/planyourvisit/hours.htm']],
  th=[42.469196,-71.348334],thn='North Bridge lot, 280 Monument Street (NPS)',brick='?',hours='Grounds daily, sunrise to sunset'),
N('old-manse-concord','The Old Manse','Concord','bos','landmark','standing',42.46837,-71.34915,'exact','open','walk','','1770',
  'A Georgian clapboard house built in 1770 for the Rev. William Emerson, Concord\'s minister, whose family watched the North Bridge fight from its upstairs windows. His grandson Ralph Waldo Emerson lived here later, and Nathaniel and Sophia Hawthorne rented it from 1842, the setting of Mosses from an Old Manse. The Emerson-Ripley family kept it until 1939, when the Trustees of Reservations took it over.',
  'Wikipedia says the house came with all its furnishings and keeps original wallpaper, woodwork and windows, with the panes in Hawthorne\'s upstairs study on which he and Sophia scratched messages. Shown by guided tour (30 minutes, on the half hour). The grounds run down to the Concord River beside the North Bridge.',
  accn='The Trustees of Reservations, 269 Monument Street. Grounds open daily, dawn to dusk, all year; fee for non-members. No public restrooms. Parking on the left of Monument Street just before the North Bridge (Trustees page). Tours are best booked ahead online.',
  links=[W('The Old Manse'),['The Trustees: The Old Manse','https://thetrustees.org/place/the-old-manse/']],
  brick='?',hours='House: Thursday to Monday 11 a.m. to 5 p.m., tours on the half hour, last 4:30; grounds daily dawn to dusk'),
N('robbins-house-concord','The Robbins House','Concord','bos','landmark','standing',42.47019,-71.34819,'exact','open','walk','','c. 1823',
  'A farmhouse of about 1823 that was home to the first free generations of the family of Caesar Robbins, who was enslaved from birth in Chelmsford and served in the Revolution. His son Peter bought it with 13 acres in 1823 for $260; his daughter Susan and her husband Jack Garrison, who had fled slavery in New Jersey, lived in the other half, and residents signed antislavery petitions from 1838 to 1861. It now stands beside the North Bridge as an interpretive centre for Concord\'s African American and antislavery history.',
  'A small two-family farmhouse with exhibits on the Robbins and Garrison families. What original fabric survives inside was not checked (interior not checked). The trust also runs Concord\'s Black Heritage Trail.',
  accn='The Robbins House (nonprofit). Free, no reservation needed; open April to October, with group tours all year (its home page; days and times were not given). Parking on the right of Monument Street at the North Bridge (its visit page).',
  links=[['The Robbins House','https://www.robbinshouse.org/'],['HMdb: The Robbins House','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=223738']],
  th=[42.469196,-71.348334],thn='North Bridge lot, Monument Street',brick='?',hours='April to October (days not given); free'),
N('david-brown-house-site-concord','Captain David Brown house site','Concord','bos','town','ruins',42.468761,-71.354731,'site','public','walk','','1644 family; house 1750s',
  'The Brown family arrived in Concord from England in 1644 and farmed this hillside above the North Bridge. In the 1750s David Brown built a new house here; on April 19, 1775 he was the 47-year-old captain of one of Concord\'s minute companies and led his men to the bridge through his own fields while his family watched.',
  'The National Park Service sign on Liberty Street points out an exposed foundation of the first Brown family house on one side and, on the other, granite marking the approximate place of David Brown\'s cellar hole.',
  accn='Minute Man National Historical Park; grounds open sunrise to sunset. Park at the North Bridge Visitor Center lot, 174 Liberty Street, and walk south along Liberty Street.',
  links=[['HMdb: Captain David Brown House Site (NPS sign)','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=18204'],MIMA],
  th=[42.47146,-71.35328],thn='North Bridge Visitor Center lot, Liberty Street',brick='no'),
N('barrett-farm-concord','Colonel James Barrett Farm','Concord','bos','landmark','standing',42.47292,-71.38077,'exact','view','drive','','1705',
  'A farmhouse built in 1705 by Benjamin Barrett. His son James, colonel of a Middlesex militia regiment, was charged with hiding the province\'s military stores, among them four brass cannon smuggled out of Boston. On April 19, 1775 about 120 British soldiers marched here from the North Bridge to search it and found nothing: the stores had been moved and the cannon sent to Groton. Save Our Heritage bought the run-down house in 2003 and restored it to its 1775 look; it joined Minute Man National Historical Park in 2012.',
  'A restored early 18th-century farmhouse at 448 Barrett\'s Mill Road, with farm fields around it. The house is opened on announced open-house days; at other times look from the road. Interior not checked.',
  accn='Minute Man National Historical Park. The park\'s own visit page for the house was "in progress" when read; no regular hours were found. A Town of Concord trailhead lot for Barrett\'s Mill Farmland is about 350 m east on Barrett\'s Mill Road (mapped in OpenStreetMap; not checked on the ground).',
  links=[W('Col. James Barrett Farm'),['NPS: Colonel James Barrett House','https://www.nps.gov/mima/learn/historyculture/colonel-james-barrett-house.htm']],
  th=[42.47341,-71.37652],thn='Barrett\'s Mill trailhead lot',brick='some'),
# ---- Concord centre ----
N('wright-tavern-concord','Wright\'s Tavern','Concord','bos','hotel','standing',42.46012,-71.34873,'exact','view','walk','','1747',
  'Built in 1747 by Ephraim Jones and kept in April 1775 by Amos Wright. Committees of the Provincial Congress met here in October 1774, the minutemen gathered here when the alarm came on April 19, 1775, and British officers took refreshment here once the troops reached the square. First Parish in Concord has owned it since 1886.',
  'Red clapboards and a double-hipped roof over two storeys, on the corner of Lexington Road and Monument Square (Wikipedia). In 2016 the Concord Museum agreed with First Parish to open it for school groups and for commemorative events in April and October; whether that still holds was not checked (interior not checked).',
  accn='Owned by First Parish in Concord; look from the street. Concord Museum\'s 2016 announcement says it opens for events in April and October. Public parking along Lexington Road and on Church Green.',
  links=[W('Wright\'s Tavern'),['Concord Museum: agreement to operate the Wright Tavern (2016)','https://concordmuseum.org/news/concord-museum-signs-historic-agreement-to-operate-the-wright-tavern/']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('colonial-inn-concord','Concord\'s Colonial Inn','Concord','bos','hotel','standing',42.46171,-71.34945,'exact','open','walk','','1716; inn 1889',
  'Three houses on Monument Square joined into one in 1897; the eastern part was built in 1716 by Colonel James Minot. The middle section held provincial munitions in the Revolution, and Henry David Thoreau\'s grandfather John Thoreau bought the eastern house in 1799. It became a hotel in 1889 and took its present name in 1900.',
  'The inn\'s own page says the 1716 Main Inn keeps exposed beams, wainscoting and wide-plank floors in its 16 rooms; the Tap Room was a store room for supplies in the Revolution (Wikipedia). Its taverns and dining rooms are open to the public.',
  accn='A working inn and restaurant at 48 Monument Square. Meal times from the inn\'s own page; the square has metered street parking.',
  links=[W('Concord\'s Colonial Inn'),['Concord\'s Colonial Inn','https://www.concordscolonialinn.com/']],
  brick='no',hours='Dining Monday to Saturday 11:30 a.m. to 9 p.m., Sunday 11 a.m. to 7:30 p.m.'),
N('thoreau-alcott-house-concord','Thoreau–Alcott House','Concord','bos','landmark','standing',42.45856,-71.35783,'exact','view','walk','','1849',
  'Built in 1849 by Josiah Davis. Henry David Thoreau moved here with his family in 1850 and died here on May 6, 1862. Louisa May Alcott later bought it for her widowed sister Anna Pratt and lived here with her father Bronson Alcott; she wrote Jo\'s Boys (1886) in this house.',
  'A private house at 255 Main Street with a marker, "Thoreau Died in This House".',
  accn='Private residence: look from Main Street only.',
  links=[W('Thoreau–Alcott House'),['HMdb: Thoreau Died in This House','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=283704']],brick='no'),
N('emerson-house-concord','Ralph Waldo Emerson House ("Bush")','Concord','bos','landmark','standing',42.45753,-71.34337,'exact','open','walk','','1828',
  'Built in 1828 by the Coolidge family as a summer house ("Coolidge Castle") beside the Cambridge and Concord Turnpike. Emerson bought it in July 1835 for $3,500, moved in with his bride Lidian the day after their wedding, and lived here for the rest of his life; the Alcotts, Margaret Fuller and Thoreau were among his visitors. It opened as a museum in 1930.',
  'A four-square two-storey frame house at 18 Cambridge Turnpike. Wikipedia says the rooms remain much as Emerson had them, with original furniture and his belongings.',
  accn='Ralph Waldo Emerson Memorial Association. Wikipedia says it is open mid-April to mid-October with an admission fee; its own visitor page gave no hours when read, so hours not checked. Concord Museum is across the road.',
  links=[W('Ralph Waldo Emerson House'),['Emerson House: visitor information','https://ralphwaldoemersonhouse.org/visit-2020-1']],
  conf='mixed',brick='some',hours='?'),
N('sleepy-hollow-cemetery-concord','Sleepy Hollow Cemetery and Author\'s Ridge','Concord','bos','burial','standing',42.46398,-71.34296,'site','public','walk','cemeteries','1855',
  'A rural cemetery laid out in 1855 by Cleveland and Copeland and dedicated on September 29, 1855 with an address by Emerson, who sat on the cemetery committee. On the hill called Author\'s Ridge lie Emerson, Thoreau and his family, Nathaniel and Sophia Hawthorne, and Louisa May Alcott; the sculptor Daniel Chester French is also buried here.',
  'The graves on Author\'s Ridge, a short walk into the grounds. The cemetery is still in use.',
  accn='Town of Concord cemetery, entered from Bedford Street; a mapped lot lies inside off Sleepy Hollow Avenue. The town\'s cemetery page did not open, so hours not checked; keep to daylight.',
  links=[W('Sleepy Hollow Cemetery (Concord, Massachusetts)')],
  th=[42.4639,-71.34042],thn='Lot inside the cemetery, Sleepy Hollow Avenue',brick='?'),
# ---- Lexington Road ----
N('orchard-house-concord','Orchard House','Concord','bos','landmark','standing',42.45911,-71.3352,'exact','open','walk','','1690–1720; Alcotts 1858–1877',
  'Two early 18th-century houses on a 12-acre apple orchard that Bronson Alcott joined into one when the family moved in in 1858. Louisa May Alcott wrote and set Little Women (1868–69) here; the family stayed until 1877. It opened as a museum on May 27, 1912.',
  'Wikipedia says all the furnishings are of the mid-19th century and about three quarters belonged to the Alcotts: May Alcott\'s paintings, family china and portraits, Bronson\'s laundry rack and Louisa\'s soapstone sink. West of the house stands Bronson\'s Hillside Chapel, the Concord School of Philosophy (1879–1888). Shown by guided tour only, at 399 Lexington Road.',
  'The house where Little Women was written, with the Alcotts\' own things inside.',
  accn='Louisa May Alcott\'s Orchard House (nonprofit). Guided tours only, admission charged; timed tickets online, walk-ins taken when there is room. Gardens and grounds free. Parking in front, or in the lot on Hawthorne Lane one block away; not on Alcott Road (its visit page).',
  links=[W('Orchard House'),['Orchard House: plan your visit','https://louisamayalcott.org/visit']],
  brick='?',hours='Holiday hours 2026–27: Monday to Friday 11 a.m. to 3:30 p.m., Saturday 10 to 5, Sunday 1 to 5'),
N('wayside-concord','The Wayside','Concord','bos','landmark','standing',42.45904,-71.33301,'exact','open','walk','','c. 1717',
  'A house whose first record is in 1717. Minuteman Samuel Whitney, muster master of the Concord Minute Men, lived here in 1775. The Alcotts bought it in 1845 and called it Hillside; Nathaniel Hawthorne bought it from them in 1852 and named it The Wayside; the children\'s writer Harriett Lothrop (Margaret Sidney) later owned it and opened it in 1927. It was the first literary site the National Park Service acquired.',
  'A colonial saltbox that the Alcotts began renovating in 1845 (Wikipedia). The house is shut for the 2026 season; the grounds stay open. Interior not checked.',
  accn='Minute Man National Historical Park, 455 Lexington Road. The NPS says the house is closed for the 2026 season and expected to reopen in summer 2027; the grounds are open sunrise to sunset. Parking lot at 42.458383, -71.333338 (NPS).',
  links=[W('The Wayside'),MIMA],
  th=[42.458383,-71.333338],thn='Wayside lot (NPS)',brick='?',hours='House closed for 2026 (reopening expected summer 2027); grounds daily sunrise to sunset'),
# ---- Walden ----
N('thoreau-cabin-site-walden','Thoreau\'s cabin site, Walden Pond','Concord','bos','landmark','traces',42.44204,-71.34243,'site','public','walk','','1845–1847',
  'Henry David Thoreau built a one-room house with his own hands near the north shore of Walden Pond and lived in it from 1845 to 1847, the basis of Walden (1854). Roland Wells Robbins found the site on November 11, 1945. The pond is a National Historic Landmark in Walden Pond State Reservation.',
  'Granite posts outline the house; a fieldstone inscription says the chimney foundation lies beneath the stones there. It is about 100 yards up from the northwest shore near Wyman Meadow, half a mile from the main lot by the pond paths (HMdb). A replica of the cabin stands near the parking lot. Thoreau\'s bed, chair and desk are in the Concord Museum.',
  'The chimney stones of the Walden cabin.',
  accn='Walden Pond State Reservation (Massachusetts DCR). The main lot is on Walden Street (Route 126) across from the beach; DCR charges for parking and the lot can fill in summer. The DCR page refused this session, so hours and fees not checked.',
  links=[W('Walden Pond'),['HMdb: Thoreau\'s Cabin site','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=49548'],['HMdb: Thoreau House Replica','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=178158'],['DCR: Walden Pond State Reservation','https://www.mass.gov/locations/walden-pond-state-reservation']],
  th=[42.44002,-71.33351],thn='Walden Pond main lot, Walden Street',brick='no'),
N('pest-house-concord','Pest House (Ephraim Potter House)','Concord','bos','landmark','standing',42.44647,-71.35542,'exact','view','drive','','before 1792',
  'A farmhouse built before 1792, probably about 1752 when Ephraim Potter married Sarah Taylor, on land of the Potter family, early settlers. Potter stored provincial supplies here before the Revolution. In the 18th century it served as a pest house, where people with smallpox and other contagious illnesses were kept apart.',
  'A private old house at 158 Fairhaven Road (the National Register gives 153). Look from the road only.',
  accn='Private residence: view from Fairhaven Road only.',
  links=[W('Pest House (Concord, Massachusetts)')],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
# ---- West Concord ----
N('damon-mill-concord','Damon Mill','Concord','bos','industry','standing',42.45344,-71.40927,'exact','view','drive','','1862; site c. 1660',
  'Works on the Assabet River here go back to a bog-iron works of about 1660, then grist and saw mills. John Brown built the first textile mill in 1808; Calvin Carver Damon took it over in 1834 and invented domett cloth, a light wool-cotton flannel. The wooden mill burned on June 19, 1862 and Edward Damon rebuilt it with the architect Elbridge Boyden that year; it made cloth until the 1890s and stored apples from about 1930 to 1973.',
  'The rebuilt 1862 mill at 9 Pond Lane: red brick, four and five storeys, with a tall round brick chimney (Wikimedia photograph), rehabilitated as offices, beside its mill pond on the Assabet. Interior not checked.',
  accn='Private offices: look from Pond Lane and the mill pond. A small lot is mapped on Pond Lane; Harrington Park\'s town lot is about 600 m south.',
  links=[W('Damon Mill')],
  th=[42.45374,-71.40989],thn='Pond Lane lot',brick='yes'),
N('west-concord-union-station','West Concord (Concord Junction) Union Station','Concord','bos','rail','standing',42.45689,-71.39297,'site','view','walk','stations','1894',
  'Concord Junction station opened in 1871 where the Framingham and Lowell Railroad crossed the Fitchburg Railroad, and this union station replaced the first wooden one in January 1894. The village and station were renamed West Concord in 1927. Its interior was restored in the 1980s and the outside repainted in its original three colours in the 2000s.',
  'A one-storey L-shaped Queen Anne building that once held waiting room, freight office and baggage room under one roof, beside the platforms of the MBTA Fitchburg Line. Wikipedia says it has been a restaurant, the Club Car Cafe, since 1990; whether it is open now was not checked.',
  accn='Beside West Concord station on Commonwealth Avenue; commuter trains still run, so stay off the tracks. Look from the platforms and the street.',
  links=[W('West Concord station')],brick='?'),
# ---- Battle Road: Lincoln and Lexington ----
N('hartwell-tavern-lincoln','Hartwell Tavern','Lincoln','bos','hotel','standing',42.45401,-71.29328,'exact','open','walk','','1732–1733',
  'Built in 1732–33 for Ephraim and Elizabeth Hartwell on the Bay Road; Ephraim was licensed as an innkeeper in 1756 and the family kept an "ordinary" here until 1787. On April 19, 1775 the British column passed it going to Concord and again in retreat; three Hartwell sons turned out as minute men. The family also enslaved a woman named Violet. The National Park Service bought it in 1967 and restored it to its 1775 look in the 1980s.',
  'A saltbox. The NPS says the main frame, the foundation, most of the walls and some flooring are original, about 60 to 70 percent of the old house, with additions of 1783 and 1830 kept. Interpreters in 18th-century dress inside in season, and musket demonstrations some days.',
  accn='Minute Man National Historical Park. Park at 112 North Great Road (Route 2A) and walk the dirt path a quarter mile north on the Battle Road Trail. Grounds open sunrise to sunset; the tavern is not wheelchair accessible (NPS).',
  links=[W('Hartwell Tavern'),['NPS: Hartwell Tavern','https://www.nps.gov/places/hartwell-tavern-1733.htm'],MIMA],
  th=[42.45135,-71.29157],thn='Hartwell Tavern lot, 112 North Great Road',brick='?',hours='Thursday, Saturday and Sunday 10 a.m. to 4:30 p.m., through October 31, 2026'),
N('samuel-hartwell-house-site-lincoln','Samuel Hartwell house site (standing chimney)','Lincoln','bos','landmark','ruins',42.45273,-71.29103,'site','public','walk','','c. 1695; burned 1968',
  'A house built in the late 1690s, the childhood home of Ephraim Hartwell. In 1775 it was home to his son Samuel, a sergeant in the Lincoln minute men, and Samuel\'s wife Mary, who later described the British column marching past and the burial of regulars killed nearby. After nearly forty years as a restaurant it was destroyed by an electrical fire in 1968.',
  'The central chimney still stands on the old house site beside the Battle Road Trail, about 700 feet east of Hartwell Tavern (NPS; Wikipedia).',
  'A colonial house reduced to its central chimney.',
  accn='Minute Man National Historical Park; grounds open sunrise to sunset. Park at the Hartwell Tavern lot, 112 North Great Road, and walk east on the Battle Road Trail.',
  links=[['NPS: Samuel Hartwell House, circa 1695','https://www.nps.gov/places/samuel-hartwell-house-circa-1695.htm'],W('Hartwell Tavern')],
  th=[42.45135,-71.29157],thn='Hartwell Tavern lot, 112 North Great Road',brick='?'),
N('ebenezer-fiske-house-site','Ebenezer Fiske house site','Lexington','bos','landmark','ruins',42.448344,-71.257137,'site','public','walk','','1674',
  'David Fiske III built his house at the foot of Fiske Hill in 1674; his son Ebenezer inherited the farm in 1729. On April 19, 1775 the retreating British fought their way over the hill; wounded soldiers were left in the house, where Dr. Joseph Fiske treated them in the parlour, and three of the four died. Archaeologists have found a large central fireplace.',
  'A jagged stone foundation at the east end of the Battle Road Trail, with NPS signs, off Wood Street (HMdb files the sign under Lincoln); a well nearby is where Acton minuteman James Hayward and a British soldier shot each other (NPS; HMdb).',
  accn='Minute Man National Historical Park; grounds open sunrise to sunset. The NPS lists parking and a trailhead here, off Wood Street; a mapped NPS lot for the Fiske Hill loop is about 550 m west.',
  links=[['NPS: Ebenezer Fiske House Site','https://www.nps.gov/places/ebenezer-fiske-house-site.htm'],['HMdb: Ebenezer Fiske House Site','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=18282']],
  th=[42.44721,-71.26379],thn='Fiske Hill lot (NPS)',brick='no'),
# ---- Bedford ----
N('job-lane-house-bedford','Job Lane House','Bedford','bos','landmark','standing',42.51287,-71.28427,'exact','open','drive','','c. 1713',
  'A saltbox built about 1713 by Job Lane, one of Bedford\'s first settlers, on land his grandfather of the same name had bought from Governor John Winthrop in 1664. It was later the Dutton family\'s farm. The Town of Bedford owns it and the Friends of the Job Lane House run it as an 18th-century house museum.',
  'A two-and-a-half-storey frame house on a stone foundation (Wikipedia). The museum\'s page names murals by Rufus Porter, an herb garden, and a 1720s-style barn raised by volunteers. It opens for tours at seasonal events; interior otherwise not checked.',
  accn='Town of Bedford; run by the Friends of the Job Lane House, 295 North Road. Its page lists seasonal events and tours for 2026 but no regular hours.',
  links=[W('Job Lane House'),['Job Lane Farm Museum','https://joblanefarmmuseum.org/']],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
]
